; SET-MAR and CLEAR-MAR over a range of words (sys/sys/qmisc.lisp; contract
; G3 revision 14, 10.10): each page from the range's first word's to its last
; word's is visited once, the addresses ordered unsigned (SI::MAP-MAR-PAGES).
; The old loops stepped #o200 words and compared signed: past 2^31 they ended
; at once (rev14-addresses tests that), and on any machine they could miss
; the page holding the last word when it lay in that page's first 127 words,
; by LOW modulo 128.  Here the range runs from 28 words below a page boundary
; to 50 words past it, so the old loop visited only the first page, and a
; write of the last word did not trap while the page was in core.  Run with
;   --compile --files sys/sys2/lmmac.lisp,sys/sys/qmisc.lisp,tools/system-check/lisp/mar-range.lisp
; on any band.  The first, second and fourth cases are controls: a write of
; the first word traps, a write outside the range does not, and none once the
; MAR is cleared.  System 2001 without the change fails the third (measured).
(progn (mar-set 100. 20.) (mar-trap-p 100.)) => T
(prog1 (mar-trap-p 130.) (si:clear-mar)) => NIL
(let ((low (- (mar-index (mar-page-after 100.)) 28.))) (mar-set low 79.) (prog1 (mar-trap-p (+ low 78.)) (si:clear-mar))) => T
(let ((low (- (mar-index (mar-page-after 100.)) 28.))) (mar-set low 79.) (si:clear-mar) (or (mar-trap-p low) (mar-trap-p (+ low 78.)))) => NIL
; Two writes in one SET-MAR (sys/eh/eh.lisp, RESTORE-MAR-MODE-FROM-FOOTHOLD):
; the error handler signals the break in the erring stack group by RUN-SG,
; which turns the stack group's MAR off while the handlers run, and only the
; return to the error handler restored it.  A handler that leaves by a throw,
; as condition-case's does in mar-trap-p, left the MAR off: the next write
; did not trap, (si:mar-mode) said NIL, and %MAR-LOW, %MAR-HIGH and the
; pages' trap status stayed set.  Check that change with
;   --compile --files sys/eh/eh.lisp,tools/system-check/lisp/mar-range.lisp
; The second of these is a control: a break proceeded with :no-action keeps
; the MAR on and does the write.  System 2002's revision-14 band without the
; change fails the first and the third (measured), and its second case above
; passes only because the MAR is off by then.
(progn (mar-set 100. 20.) (prog1 (list (mar-trap-p 100.) (mar-trap-p 101.) (si:mar-mode)) (si:clear-mar))) => (T T :WRITE)
(progn (mar-set 100. 20.) (prog1 (list (mar-proceed-p 100.) (mar-proceed-p 101.) (aref *mar-array* 101.) (si:mar-mode)) (si:clear-mar))) => (T T 1 :WRITE)
(progn (mar-set 100. 20.) (prog1 (list (mar-proceed-p 100.) (mar-trap-p 101.) (mar-trap-p 102.)) (si:clear-mar))) => (T T T)
